Nscale release v1.2.1-rc2-nscale.1
===================================

This branch, `release/v1.2.1-nscale`, is the Nscale build of Agent Router (Envoy AI Gateway) v1.2.1-rc2. It is
upstream's v1.2 patch series plus fixes the fork needs that upstream has not released on v1.2 yet.

Release tag: `nscale/v1.2.1-rc2-nscale.1`. Pushing the tag runs `.github/workflows/release.yaml`, which publishes:

| Artifact          | Location                                                           |
|-------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------|
| Controller image  | `registry.nscale.com/envir/ai-gateway-controller:v1.2.1-rc2-nscale.1` |
| Extproc image     | `registry.nscale.com/envir/ai-gateway-extproc:v1.2.1-rc2-nscale.1`    |
| Helm chart        | `oci://registry.nscale.com/helm-charts/ai-gateway-helm`, version `v1.2.1-rc2-nscale.1` |
| CRDs Helm chart   | `oci://registry.nscale.com/helm-charts/ai-gateway-crds-helm`, version `v1.2.1-rc2-nscale.1` |
| GitHub release    | Draft release on the tag, with the charts and `aigw` binaries attached |

Images carry the `org.opencontainers.image.revision` label with the commit they were built from. The chart is
rewritten to point at the Harbor images before it is packaged.


How the branch is built
-----------------------

The branch is four layers. Commit SHAs are on this branch; "from" gives the commit it was cherry-picked from
(`git cherry-pick -x` records it in each commit message).

```
upstream release/v1.2 (v1.2.1-rc2, 45ccd9fe)
  + fork CI                      4 commits, from the fork's main
  + upstream main-only fixes     8 commits, from upstream main
  + embedding passthrough fixes  9 commits, the fork's own
  + release tag format           1 commit
```


1. Base: upstream v1.2.1-rc2
----------------------------

Upstream `release/v1.2` at `45ccd9fe` ("envoy 1.39.6"), tagged `v1.2.1-rc2`. Over v1.2.0 it adds:

- The MCP proxy listens on `127.0.0.1` only. It used to bind every interface, so any pod that could reach port
  9856 could set the headers Envoy sets after enforcing the MCPRoute security policy, and skip that policy.
- Malformed 2xx upstream responses (Anthropic without `usage`, null Gemini, image generation or Bedrock Converse
  bodies) no longer panic the external processor.
- MCP session IDs work only on the MCPRoute that created them.
- `promptSelector` is enforced on `prompts/get` and `completion/complete`.
- The controller reads at most 1 MiB of an MCPRoute's OAuth authorization server metadata.
- MCP session encryption seeds are delivered in the filter config Secret instead of as command-line arguments,
  so they no longer appear in Pod specs.
- Debug logs redact `api-key` and `x-amz-security-token`, and header mutations in immediate responses.
- Controller replicas retry on conflict when patching the admission webhook's `caBundle`.
- Go 1.27.3 and Envoy Proxy 1.39.6.

Upstream's own notes are in `release-notes/v1.2.1.md`. Upgrading from v1.2.0 rolls Gateway pods that serve
MCPRoutes once, and an MCP session ID reused on a different MCPRoute now returns 400.


2. Fork CI
----------

These make CI publish to Harbor from `nscale/v*` tags and turn off the jobs that only work in the upstream
repository. The workflow files match the fork's `main`, except for the tag format change in layer 5.

- `e1201e6d`, from `0378a7a6`: gate upstream-only workflows.
  Why: v1.2.0's CI pushes images and the chart on every push to a `release/**` branch, using upstream's
  registry credentials, and fails unittest on the Codecov upload because the fork has no `CODECOV_TOKEN`.
  Without this commit, pushing this branch would try to publish to upstream's registries. It also adds
  `hack/nscale/set-chart-images.sh`, which points the published chart at the fork's images.

- `fbab7528`, from `b0777d9e`: publish images and charts to Harbor instead of GHCR.
  Why: Nscale deployments pull from `registry.nscale.com`. The commit also adds the safety checks:
  `hack/nscale/push-charts.sh` will not overwrite a published chart version with different contents, and
  the image push stops if the tag is already published from another commit.

- `ae4178db`, from `38e53aea`: finish partial GitHub releases on re-run.
  Why: a failed asset upload used to leave a draft release that a re-run skipped, so the release could not
  be completed. A re-run now uploads only the missing assets. The commit's change to the upstream-sync
  workflow is left out, because that workflow only belongs on the fork's `main`.

- `bd92cbdb`, from `4a46575f`: log in to Harbor for charts with the repository's chart robot.
  Why: the release job cannot push charts without it. The org's Harbor robot secrets are not shared with
  public repositories, and the image robot has no access to the `helm-charts` project.


3. Upstream main-only fixes
---------------------------

Bug fixes merged to upstream `main` after v1.2.0 that upstream has not backported to `release/v1.2`. Each is
self-contained and applied without conflicts. Upstream `main` features were left out (see "Not included").

- `d086ad4a`, from `f2a14b7c`: add Claude Sonnet 5.5 to the structured output and effort model lists.
  Why: when an OpenAI-schema request is translated for an Anthropic model on AWS Bedrock or GCP Vertex AI,
  the gateway forwards `response_format` JSON schemas and `reasoning_effort` only for models on these lists.
  Without this commit both are silently dropped for Sonnet 5.5.

- `5ee7bfe5`, from `31ee356f`: add one InferencePool ext proc filter per pool, not per route match.
  Why: a pool referenced by N route matches got N identically named endpoint-picker filters on the listener,
  because the duplicate check missed filters added in the same pass.

- `547b6fc3`, from `35307e5e`: keep Anthropic streaming overload errors in-band.
  Why: Anthropic can answer a streaming request with HTTP 200 and report `overloaded_error` later in the
  stream. The gateway turned that into a generic HTTP 500, or cut off the stream, so clients could not tell
  an overload from a gateway failure and back off. It now sends the overload as a terminal SSE error event.

- `e1435c1e`, from `ffdb12ea`: apply QuotaPolicy changes to the Envoy config without a restart.
  Why: an updated QuotaPolicy did not reach the data plane until both the controller and the Envoy pod were
  restarted. Envoy Gateway does not watch QuotaPolicy, and the controller's attempt to force re-translation
  produced an identical HTTPRoute, so nothing changed.

- `0b5a5e48`, from `81c3ccc4`: stop charging a quota hit at request time.
  Why: request-time rate limit checks left `hits_addend` unset, which the rate limit service treats as 1. A
  request that used 28 tokens was charged 29, so token quotas ran out early.

- `1ccbb632`, from `1c98cc80`: add Claude Haiku 5.5 to the structured output and effort model lists.
  Why: the same as the Sonnet 5.5 commit above, for Haiku 5.5.

- `19e0c102`, from `f89fa2b3`: keep the query string on Anthropic passthrough.
  Why: the passthrough translator rewrote `:path` and dropped the client's query string. Claude Code calls
  `/anthropic/v1/messages?beta=true`, so Anthropic-schema backends never received `?beta=true`.

- `4e2a8f8f`, from `76f60022`: forward ping events on the AWS Anthropic messages path.
  Why: Bedrock holds back the deltas of a large tool call until the model finishes, and sends `ping` events in
  the meantime. The gateway dropped the pings, so a client could see no bytes for minutes. Clients with an idle
  timeout, such as Claude Code at roughly 3 to 5 minutes, gave up and retried a request that would have
  succeeded.


4. Embedding passthrough fixes
------------------------------

The fork's fixes for memory use on OpenAI and Azure OpenAI embedding passthrough, proposed upstream from the
`fix/embedding-passthrough-memory-upstream` branch. The "from" commits are on that branch. They were picked
because embedding responses with large vectors used several times the response size in extproc memory, even
when the gateway only needed the response's `model` and `usage`.

- `91b5d861`, from `9be4dd4c`: escape user-facing error JSON.
  Why: error bodies the gateway returns itself were built with string formatting, so a message containing a
  quote, backslash or newline produced invalid JSON for the client. It is a separate fix, but the embedding
  branch was built on top of it, so it is picked with the rest.

- `0e1ae3b1`, from `f81a7760`: avoid decoding vectors in embeddings passthrough.
  Why: this is the main memory fix. When spans are not recording, the response is decoded for `model` and
  `usage` only, skipping the vectors instead of materialising them.

- `ef0cd586`, from `8b0619fa`: keep case-sensitive embedding metadata decoding.
  Why: the sparse path uses Go's standard decoder, which also matches `MODEL`, `USAGE` and other case variants.
  The gateway's normal decoder is case-sensitive. This commit decodes field by field, so the sparse path accepts
  exactly what the normal path accepts.

- `3ad169d4`, from `c7f4947e`: make embedding trace capture best effort.
  Why: a vector or trace-only field that fails to decode should drop that value from the trace, not reject a
  response the client would otherwise receive.

- `8a92427d`, from `9a14104c`: make embedding vector validation configurable.
  Why: full vector validation is the main memory cost. This adds `extProc.validateEmbeddingVectors`, so a
  deployment can turn it off and keep only JSON syntax and `model`/`usage` validation.

- `ad778cb4`, from `a90b9f3e`: make passthrough embedding trace vectors optional.
  Why: with validation off, tracing still decodes vectors to record them. This adds
  `extProc.traceEmbeddingVectors`, so traces can keep `model` and token usage without decoding vectors.

- `3b701726`, from `19cd7a1d`: document embedding validation and trace memory costs.
  Why: operators need the defaults, trade-offs and benchmark figures before turning either setting off.

- `b5c76d26`, from `5a1b7ac8`: drop the generated chart README.
  Why: `8a92427d` had added a chart README generated by helm-docs from v1.0.0 values. Nothing in the
  repository generates or checks it, so it would go stale; the settings are documented in the gateway config
  guide instead.

- `c84045b4`, from `91a49be9`: use the chart's comment style for the embedding values.
  Why: keeps `values.yaml` consistent with the rest of the chart.

Both new settings default to `true`, which keeps upstream behaviour. To cut embedding memory use, turn them off
in the chart values:

```yaml
extProc:
  # Validate vectors in embedding responses. With false, only JSON syntax and model/usage are validated.
  # A 100 MiB response benchmark allocated about 2.66 GiB with validation, versus 256 MiB without it.
  validateEmbeddingVectors: false
  # Capture vectors in traces. Only applies when validateEmbeddingVectors is false.
  traceEmbeddingVectors: false
```

| Chart value                        | Controller flag                      | Extproc flag                |
|------------------------------------|--------------------------------------|-----------------------------|
| `extProc.validateEmbeddingVectors` | `--extProcValidateEmbeddingVectors`  | `-validateEmbeddingVectors` |
| `extProc.traceEmbeddingVectors`    | `--extProcTraceEmbeddingVectors`     | `-traceEmbeddingVectors`    |

The controller passes the extproc flags only when they are `false`, so deployments that keep the defaults are not
rolled on upgrade. The full description is in `site/docs/capabilities/gateway-config.md` and
`site/docs/capabilities/observability/tracing.md`.


5. Release tag format
---------------------

`2d0eb196` lets `release.yaml` accept a tag built on an upstream release candidate. Tags must match
`nscale/v<major>.<minor>.<patch>[-rc<m>]-nscale.<n>`, for example `nscale/v1.2.0-nscale.1` or
`nscale/v1.2.1-rc2-nscale.1`.


Not included
------------

Upstream `main` also has features after v1.2.0 that this branch leaves out: dynamic, weekly, monthly and yearly
quota windows, cross-namespace quota backends, the OpenAI decision API, OpenAI workload identity federation, and
the removal of the unused `GCPWorkloadIdentityProvider` API type. It also leaves out docs, site and test-only
changes.


Cutting the next release
------------------------

When upstream tags the final v1.2.1, or later v1.2 patches, rebuild the branch on the new upstream `release/v1.2`
and reapply layers 2 to 5. Drop any layer 3 commit that upstream has backported, and any layer 4 commit that
upstream has merged. Then increment the `-nscale.<n>` number, or reset it to 1 for a new upstream version, and
update this file.


When the fork retires
---------------------

Each layer is temporary, and each has a named replacement upstream:

- Upstream main-only fixes: the first upstream release that contains them, either a v1.2 backport or v1.3.0.
- Embedding passthrough fixes: upstream merging and releasing the ports on this repository's
  `fix/error-json-escaping-upstream` and `fix/embedding-passthrough-memory-upstream` branches.
- Any later fix layer: the upstream release that contains it, as for the main-only fixes.
- Fork CI and release tag format: nothing. They exist only to publish the other layers.

The fork retires when one upstream release contains every fix layer this branch carries. The base layer then
installs upstream's charts and images at that release, no further `nscale/v*` tag is cut, and this branch and
its Harbor artifacts are kept only until no environment pins them.

Until then, each new release of this branch drops the commits upstream has released, as described in "Cutting
the next release". A branch with only the fork CI and release tag format layers left is the signal to retire it.
